WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.150
Ransomware demonstrates how an enterprise cyber incident can become an operational crisis.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.360
An attacker does not need to reprogram a controller to stop production.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.555
Loss of identity services, virtualization, engineering workstations, scheduling,

00:00:12.555 --> 00:00:13.520
quality systems,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.960
or operator support applications may make continued operation unsafe or impractical.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.760
Organizations sometimes shut down proactively because they cannot confirm where the

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.045
intrusion reached or whether process visibility remains trustworthy.

00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:28.005
That decision protects operations, but it also creates downtime.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:31.550
The distinction between business systems and control systems still matters for

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.300
investigation,

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.159
yet the recovery plan must account for their dependencies.

00:00:35.159 --> 00:00:37.840
OT recovery is more complex than restoring files.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.140
Teams may need to rebuild trusted workstations,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.795
validate controller logic and configurations, confirm network boundaries,

00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:47.070
reconcile physical process state, and sequence equipment startup.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.920
Vendors and engineers may be required.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.300
A recovered server is not proof that the operation is safe to resume.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.830
Segmentation reduces the ability of ransomware to spread from user networks into site

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:57.210
operations.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.940
Controlled identity and administration limit privilege.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.880
Reliable backups should include controller programs, device configurations,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.925
HMI and SCADA applications, historians, servers, and essential documentation.

00:01:07.925 --> 00:01:10.700
Backups need protected storage and practiced restoration.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.200
Response planning should define who can isolate networks, pause production,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.550
validate equipment, communicate with partners, and authorize restart.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.290
Monitor for activity crossing IT and OT boundaries,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.740
but interpret alerts with operational context.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.390
Resilience comes from architecture, preparation, and coordinated recovery,

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.480
not from assuming that ransomware will remain on one side of an organizational chart.
